    <h2 class="page-title">About</h2>

    <article class="prose">
      <p>Ella Amitay Sadovsky<br>Born in Kibbutz Gesher, Israel<br>Works in Tel Aviv</p>
      <h3>CV</h3>
      <p><a href="/pdfs/cv_ella_amitay_sadovsky_2026.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Ella Amitay Sadovsky (2026, PDF)</a></p>
      <h2>Artist Statement</h2>
      <p>My work explores the lived reality of women's lives, as it manifests in both public and domestic spaces, with a particular focus on the gap between utopia and the reality of everyday life. Creating stop-motion video allows me to add a layer of movement to an already multi-layered painting practice.</p>
      <p>My last museum show at the Petach Tikva Museum of Art was the exhibition <em>"Suddenly, Just When You Thought It Was Gone"</em> — a stop-motion video installation based on painting with animated projection layered onto it. The work tells the story of my grandmother, who remained alone on the front line defending her kibbutz against the Arab Legion and the Iraqi army in 1948. Alongside her story are the memories of my father, who was smuggled as a child during the night of the attack to a monastery in Haifa. Also included is my personal story as a child living during the War of Attrition (1967–1970) in an underground shelter, sleeping on foldable iron cots, with almost no exposure to daylight.</p>
      <p>The installation <em>Out There</em> (2024), presented at the Wilfrid Israel Museum of Asian Art, consists of three theatrical tableaux. It deals with the Sisyphean nature of female existence, the woman's relationship with her environment, and her journey toward inner freedom, unbound by conventions and prejudices.</p>
      <p>In the project <em>Without Leaving Home</em> (2024), exhibited at Gordon Gallery, I presented a series of paintings that examine the tension between the complex domestic space and the exterior landscape seen through the windows — a view that expresses the possibility of a different gaze, or a dream of escape.</p>
      <p>In my earlier video work <em>Seven</em>, I created a video installation simulating a home inhabited by a person who observes what happens inside and through the windows. The installation was awarded the Ann and Ari Rosenblatt Prize for Visual Art (2016).</p>
      <p>My first stop-motion video work, <em>Quiet Island</em> (2012), was a developing painting created on a public bulletin board in Jerusalem, responding to the stories of women who passed by. Around 5,000 paintings were painted one over the other during about six months, forming a collective voice of many women who shared their life stories throughout the project.</p>

      <h2>Bio</h2>
      <p>Born in Israel, Amitay Sadovsky has been working as an artist and scientist for most of her life. She has exhibited nationally and internationally at venues such as the University of California at Berkeley (CA, USA), the Israel Museum (Jerusalem), the Tel Aviv Museum of Art, the Petach Tikva Museum of Art, The Open Museum (Tefen, Israel), The Weizmann Institute of Science (Rehovot, Israel), and The Janco Dada Museum (Ein Hod, Israel). She has received numerous prizes and fellowships as an artist and as a scientist from the National Science Foundation, USA (2000–2003), Ministry of Culture and Sports prize, Israel (2012), The MacDowell Colony Fellowship, USA (2015, 2025), and the Ann and Ari Rosenblatt Prize for Visual Art (2016). Two books of her work were published (2012, 2014). In addition to painting she creates stop motion video performances. She is currently teaching at Shenkar College of Engineering and Design and at the Technion–Israel Institute of Technology. Amitay Sadovsky holds a PhD from The Weizmann Institute of Science, a BSc and MSc from the Technion, and a BFA and MFA from Bezalel Academy of Art and Design. She did her postdoctoral research at the University of California at Berkeley (CA, USA) and studied for her BFA at CCA (CA, USA). Amitay Sadovsky's work is included in public and private national and international collections, including the Israel Museum, the Tel Aviv Museum of Art, The Open Museum, The Janco Dada Museum, the Dubi Shiff Collection, and Luciano Benetton's Imago Mundi Collection.</p>

